libQEx uses the cmake build system.

Dependencies
------------

libQEx depends on OpenMesh (http://openmesh.org/). Any OpenMesh version from 3
onwards works; because the OpenMesh headers are compiled in C++17 mode (see
below), current releases such as OpenMesh 11 are fine. On macOS, OpenMesh is
available from Homebrew:

    brew install open-mesh

The sources are built as C++17. The top-level CMakeLists.txt sets
CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD accordingly - QEx itself and modern OpenMesh headers both
compile cleanly in that mode, while the C++98 mode used historically does not.
CMake 3.10 or newer is required (CMake 4.x rejects the historic
"cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 2.6)").

If you want to build the unit tests, you additionally need the googletest
sources and pass their location via -D GTEST_DIR. Homebrew does not install
googletest sources, so a checkout of
https://github.com/google/googletest is the simplest way to get them.

Building libQEx
---------------

# Clone libQEx
git clone https://github.com/hcebke/libQEx.git libQEx
cd libQEx

# Create a build directory
mkdir build
cd build

# Initialize the build directory. OpenMesh is found automatically when it is
# installed in a standard location, including Homebrew's /opt/homebrew
# (Apple Silicon) and /usr/local (Intel) prefixes.
cmake ..

# If OpenMesh lives somewhere unusual, point CMake at it explicitly:
cmake -D OPENMESH_INCLUDE_DIR=/path/to/OpenMesh/include \
    -D OPENMESH_LIBRARY_DIR=/path/to/OpenMesh/lib ..

# Alternatively, initialize the build directory, including the unit tests
cmake -D BUILD_UNIT_TESTS=true -D GTEST_DIR=/path/to/googletest ..

# Build
make

# Build the documentation (requires Doxygen)
make qex_doc

# If you have built the unit tests, execute them
./tests/QEx_tests

# Try the demo program
./demo/cmdline_tool/cmdline_tool --help

# To get started with the API, have a look at the API Doc ...
firefox ./doc/html/index.html

# ... and, even better, at the sources of the command line tool.
vim ../demo/cmdline_tool/main.cpp
